ConstructionAssemblyDefinition
- assembly_id/version/h

## Summary

Broad product-boundary review of HTDT `main@db35b4fcaa3776930274077b6432cdfb066458bb` and the current open roadmap found that the product has expanded far beyond the original:

```text
Room CAD
+ acoustic measurement
+ prediction
+ placement optimization
```

HTDT now has/plans significant capability across:

- equipment/source/playback chain;
- acoustic treatment;
- arbitrary-room acoustics;
- auralization;
- optimization/robustness/system expansion;
- installation/as-built/commissioning;
- video geometry/HDR/colorimetry;
- lighting scenes;
- rack/electrical/thermal planning;
- sound isolation;
- BOM/procurement;
- Capture/Mission workflow;
- project history/decision/evidence management.

Most individual issues correctly include local non-goals such as:

> not BIM  
> not electrical-code engineering  
> not HVAC design  
> not a LUT generator  
> not a full nonlinear transducer solver  
> not an ERP system

Those local boundaries are good.

The missing piece is a **single cross-domain product charter** that answers:

> What does HTDT itself own deeply, what does it coordinate in bounded form, and what should remain delegated to specialist tools?

Without that contract, many individually reasonable features can cumulatively turn HTDT into an unfocused combination of:

- CAD/BIM;
- REW replacement;
- room-acoustics solver;
- display calibration suite;
- electrical design software;
- HVAC tool;
- lighting-design package;
- procurement/ERP;
- project-management system.

The risk is product complexity, duplicated engines, confused GUI ownership and endless validation scope.

## Goal

Define and maintain a versioned **HTDT Product Domain Boundary Charter**.

The central principle should be:

> HTDT owns the theater Digital Twin, exact cross-domain authority/lineage, theater-specific design comparison and closed-loop verification.  
> HTDT integrates specialist domain evidence/results where that is more appropriate than recreating mature specialist software.

This charter should guide new features and issue review.

It should not delete useful adjacent-domain features that fit the Digital Twin.

## 1. Define three product-depth tiers

Every significant domain belongs to one of three explicit tiers.

### Tier A — Core HTDT-owned domain

HTDT provides native first-class authoring/evaluation because the capability is central to the Digital Twin and cross-domain workflow.

Expected examples:

- project/Scene/SystemVariant identity;
- theater room geometry;
- theater entity placement;
- speaker/listener/screen/projector physical layout;
- equipment/source/system topology identity;
- measurement ↔ Scene binding;
- prediction authority orchestration;
- multi-objective/robust comparison;
- current/proposed/as-built/measured lifecycle;
- installation geometry/handoff;
- cross-domain evidence/provenance;
- commissioning reconciliation.

Tier A still may use OSS libraries/solvers internally.

“Owned” does not mean “write every numerical kernel ourselves”.

### Tier B — Bounded theater-adjacent capability

HTDT owns only the subset needed to coordinate the theater Digital Twin.

Examples:

#### Video
Own:
- projector/display geometry;
- image aperture/sightlines;
- presentation modes;
- imported/measured photometric/colorimetric commissioning;
- exact picture-mode/settings evidence.

Do not automatically own:
- full professional display-calibration engine;
- LUT-generation suite;
- pattern-generator ecosystem.

#### Lighting
Own:
- theater fixture/zone identity;
- operating scenes relevant to playback/measurement;
- ambient-light evidence;
- bias-light commissioning.

Do not own:
- architectural lighting design;
- photometric building simulation;
- electrical lighting-control engineering.

#### Electrical/rack
Own:
- equipment inventory;
- documented power/load arithmetic;
- PDU/circuit assignment;
- physical rack layout;
- documented ventilation/service clearance.

Do not own:
- code compliance;
- breaker/conductor sizing;
- struct

## Summary

Deep installation/product-boundary review of HTDT `main@db35b4fcaa3776930274077b6432cdfb066458bb` found a major gap between “the equipment fits geometrically in the room” and “the equipment can plausibly be installed at that location”.

HTDT now has/plans strong coverage for:

- Room CAD geometry;
- body/collision geometry (#464);
- speaker/projector/screen placement;
- equipment definitions;
- cable/wiring planning;
- rack infrastructure (#562);
- dimensional constraints (#618);
- installation drawing output (#644);
- installation/commissioning verification (#520).

But the model still lacks bounded installation constraints such as:

- in-wall speaker cutout must fit between framing/known obstructions;
- in-ceiling speaker location conflicts with a known joist or unavailable cavity;
- projector mount requires an approved mounting region/substrate;
- screen bracket holes/rails require mounting support;
- cable entry needs a reachable route/penetration zone;
- equipment requires rear cavity/depth and connector access;
- a planned mounting surface is known only as drywall and structural support is UNKNOWN.

This is a frequent real-world reason a theoretically good theater layout cannot be built as planned.

## Goal

Add a bounded **Installation Feasibility Authority**.

It should combine explicit product mounting requirements with explicit observed/imported construction constraints and report:

```text
SUPPORTED / CONFLICT / UNKNOWN / NOT_APPLICABLE
```

with reasons.

Do **not** make HTDT a structural-engineering, electrical-code, or BIM application.

## 1. Separate acoustic surface from installation substrate

An acoustic/visual surface classification does not prove mounting capability.

Keep distinct:

```text
Semantic/acoustic surface
- drywall / boundary / acoustic material behavior

Installation substrate / construction evidence
- known framing
- known solid backing
- known cavity
- observed obstruction
- unknown
```

“Gypsum drywall” must not imply “safe to mount 20 kg here”.

## 2. Construction evidence authority

Represent only what is actually known.

Possible records:

```text
MountingSupportObservation
- host surface ref
- region geometry
- support kind
- depth/thickness where explicitly known
- orientation/spacing where explicitly observed/imported
- source/evidence
- uncertainty
```

Support kinds may include:

- solid structural substrate;
- stud/framing member;
- joist;
- blocking/backing;
- hollow cavity;
- known service/obstruction;
- unknown.

Names can evolve; semantics matter more than taxonomy.

Do not infer hidden framing from standard construction practice.

## 3. Framing/grid observations

Allow bounded representation of:

- one observed stud/joist;
- repeated framing pattern only when explicitly imported/attested;
- unknown sections.

If spacing is estimated:

- label estimated;
- retain evidence/method;
- do not extrapolate indefinitely without a declared valid region.

## 4. Cavity authority

For in-wall/in-ceiling products, support an explicit cavity/available-volume observation.

Examples:

- known cavity depth;
- known clear rectangular region;
- insulation/obstruction noted;
- unknown depth.

Do not treat wall thickness or Room mesh as proof of an empty cavity.

## 5. Equipment mounting requirements

Reusable requirements belong to EquipmentDefinition/product installation data where supported.

Examples:

### In-wall/in-ceiling speaker
- cutout geometry;
- minimum cavity depth;
- optional keepout around clamps;
- required orientation;
- supported wall/ceiling thickness range if documented.

### Projector mount
- mounting interface/pattern reference;
