## Summary

Deep workflow review of HTDT `main@db35b4fcaa3776930274077b6432cdfb066458bb` found that cross-workspace navigation is much less expressive than the authority model it now needs to navigate.

The current canonical transport is essentially:

```python
WorkspaceDeepLink(
    workspace,
    section: str | None,
    entity_id: str | None,
)
```

and mounted workspaces expose only a generic:

```text
on_entity_requested(str)
```

This was sufficient when most jumps meant “open Room and select one Scene entity”.

It is no longer sufficient for a product that now contains:

- SceneRevision;
- SystemVariant;
- Capture delivery/revision;
- measurement/dataset/campaign;
- EquipmentDefinition and installed equipment instance;
- treatment/material/instrument definitions;
- prediction result;
- optimization candidate;
- standards/commissioning evaluation;
- authority/evidence records;
- project checkpoints/history;
- long-running activity results.

A string `entity_id` cannot say what namespace an ID belongs to, which revision/variant it is valid under, what the destination should focus, or where Back should return.

## User-visible failure mode

Without a stronger navigation contract, individual features will invent local behavior.

Examples:

- “Why is this result stale?” links to Room, but not the exact SceneRevision/evidence dependency.
- Capture Inbox promotes an entity and then cannot return the user to the staged delivery.
- an EquipmentDefinition link and an installed physical instance share an identifier-looking string but require different surfaces;
- an optimization candidate opens Room but loses candidate-preview context;
- History/Authority Graph links open a workspace but do not preserve the source comparison;
- Overview cards select a page but a later refresh loses the selected object.

This is a product coherence problem, not merely a URI problem.

## Goal

Create a **typed, scope-aware navigation target and navigation-context contract**.

Navigation must be able to express:

> Open this exact user-facing object, in this exact project/revision/variant context, at the surface capable of showing it, while preserving a return path.

Do not turn navigation records into domain authority.

## 1. Replace generic entity_id with typed targets

Conceptually:

```text
NavigationTarget
- scope
- project/document ref where required
- kind
- stable object ref
- optional exact revision/system-variant context
- preferred surface/context
- presentation intent
```

Example target kinds may include:

- scene_entity;
- scene_revision;
- system_variant;
- measurement;
- measurement_campaign;
- prediction_result;
- optimization_candidate;
- capture_delivery;
- equipment_definition;
- installed_equipment_instance;
- treatment/material/instrument definition;
- commissioning_evaluation;
- project_checkpoint;
- activity/job;
- help topic.

Use a closed/versioned navigation vocabulary with an extension strategy.

Do not use one global untyped string namespace.

## 2. Distinguish object identity from presentation intent

The same object can be opened for different reasons.

Examples:

```text
speaker FL
- edit placement
- inspect provenance
- inspect measurement binding
- compare proposed vs as-built

prediction result
- inspect plot
- inspect assumptions
- locate producing SceneRevision
```

Target identity and presentation intent should be separate.

A destination may normalize unsupported intents to a safe inspect view.

## 3. Exact revision/variant context

A link from a historical result must not silently select the current SceneRevision.

Allow targets to pin:

- SceneRevision;
- SystemVariant;
- measurement campaign;
- evaluation/result identity.

If the object is historical:

- show historical/read-only state;
- make “Go to current” explicit.

Do not silently rebind the target to current truth.

## 4. Project scope establishment

A deep link/search/activity result may refer to a project other than the currently open one.

