## Summary

Deep product/GUI review of current HTDT `main@db35b4fcaa3776930274077b6432cdfb066458bb` found that the application shell is still architecturally frozen around exactly four project workspaces:

```text
概要 / 部屋 / 測定 / 最適化
```

That was a good simplifying decision for the original CAD-first product, but the product scope has expanded materially.

The code still encodes the old boundary directly:

- `WorkspaceId` has exactly four values;
- `WorkspaceRouter` rejects registrations that are not exactly the four canonical destinations;
- `WorkflowShellWindow` repeats the same four-destination invariant;
- the top context bar assumes every new capability can be expressed as a subsection of one of those four workspaces.

Meanwhile the planned product now includes major surfaces such as:

- project/document library (#450);
- Capture Inbox (#589);
- Application Activity Center (#603);
- Support & Diagnostics Center (#604);
- Reference Libraries (#605);
- project BOM/procurement (#639);
- installation/commissioning (#520);
- lighting scenes (#640);
- video/HDR/colorimetry (#557/#647);
- equipment/source library (#444);
- authority/history/assumption inspection (#590/#615/#620).

If every new domain is forced under Overview, Room, Measurements, Optimize, or Settings, the shell will become semantically misleading and individual workspaces will accumulate unrelated controls.

This is not a request to add many top-level tabs.

The goal is to **define a scalable information architecture with explicit application-level vs project-level product boundaries** before the next feature wave is wired into the GUI.

## Why this matters

The original four-workspace model is task-focused and easy to learn.

Its failure mode is gradual rather than catastrophic:

- Capture delivery starts living under an arbitrary project page;
- Equipment/Reference libraries get hidden under Settings even though they are productive content;
- long-running jobs appear independently inside each workspace;
- commissioning/video/install features are squeezed into Room or Measurements;
- project switching and application-wide resources have no natural owner;
- users must remember which implementation domain happens to host a feature.

That would undermine the exact workflow-first design that #118/#578 were intended to establish.

The problem is now primarily **information architecture**, not theming.

