## Summary

Deep end-to-end product review of current HTDT `main@6ad227fafc88c6f19e0bdda927114326e2d5a081` found a lifecycle gap after a design alternative has been selected.

HTDT already has/plans:
- reusable equipment/treatment authority;
- SystemVariant design alternatives;
- cable runs;
- rack/infrastructure;
- installation output;
- cost/effort scenario (#514);
- installed physical equipment instances (#569).

What is still missing is a project-level authority answering:

> What exact items does this selected theater design require, which ones are already owned, which still need to be acquired, and what was actually received/installed?

Repository/issue search found no first-class:
- bill of materials / material takeoff;
- purchase/procurement plan;
- owned-vs-required reconciliation;
- ordered/received lifecycle;
- exact design-revision -> procurement line-item traceability.

Without this layer, the workflow still jumps from:

```text
design selected
-> external spreadsheet / shopping notes
-> installation
```

and later cannot reliably explain whether an installed deviation came from a design change, a substituted product, or an unpurchased item.

## Goal

Introduce a project-scoped **Bill of Materials / Procurement Plan** derived from exact design authority.

It should remain separate from:
- volatile price/cost facts (#514);
- reusable model definitions;
- installed physical asset identity (#569);
- SceneRevision/SystemVariant truth;
- installation verification (#520).

The BOM is a reproducible implementation requirement set, not the digital twin itself.

## 1. Derive BOM from exact selected design

A BOM snapshot should bind to exact authority such as:
- SceneRevision;
- selected/applied SystemVariant where applicable;
- treatment design;
- cable-run plan;
- rack/infrastructure plan;
- screen/projector/display configuration;
- relevant operating/install profiles.

Conceptually:

```text
ProjectBOM
- bom_id / version / semantic hash
- project_id
- exact design authority refs
- line_items[]
- generation policy/version
- generated_at
```

Changing the design stales the BOM; it must not silently rewrite the old BOM.

## 2. Typed line items

Do not make every line an arbitrary string.

Candidate categories:
- loudspeaker/subwoofer;
- direct-view display;
- projector;
- projection screen;
- AVR/processor/amplifier;
- tactile actuator (#638);
- acoustic treatment;
- rack/cabinet/PDU/UPS;
- mount/bracket/stand;
- cable;
- connector/termination accessory;
- conduit;
- installation material;
- user-defined miscellaneous item.

Each line retains the exact originating design requirement where possible.

## 3. Quantity semantics

A BOM line needs explicit quantity and unit.

Examples:

```text
Speaker model X              2 each
Absorber panel Y             8 each
14 AWG speaker cable        64 m
HDMI fiber cable             1 each / 12 m
Rack shelf                   2 each
```

Do not represent cable length as quantity=64 pieces.

Use typed unit semantics.

## 4. Model requirement vs exact SKU

Support progressive resolution.

A design may initially require:

```text
2 x surround speaker meeting EquipmentDefinition X
```

or:

```text
1 x 12 m HDMI run meeting route/capability constraints
```

before an exact retail SKU/vendor is chosen.

Later procurement resolution may bind:
- exact manufacturer/model;
- exact catalog definition;
- exact user-selected SKU/merchant reference if desired.

Do not force commercial SKU identity into engineering authority.

## 5. Owned / reusable inventory

Compose with #569 InstalledEquipmentInstance.

A BOM reconciliation can classify each required line as:
- already owned and reusable;
- newly required;
- optional;
- replacement;
- spare;
- unresolved.

Example:

```text
AVR           reuse existing instance AVR-01
Front L/R     reuse existing
Height pair   acquire 2
Projector     replace existing
```

Do not charge or purchase an already-owned item merely because it appears in the BOM.

