## Summary

Broad native UX/lifecycle review of current `main@74dd2746747c9f4be5bb50f11bcc8d236a4470db` found that HTDT has a robust low-level worker abstraction but still treats most asynchronous work as **workspace-owned state**.

Current architecture includes:

- `NativeWorkerPool` with cooperative cancellation and bounded shutdown;
- prediction-specific worker pools;
- Optimize search/extended-search/REW worker pools;
- job guards that prevent stale result application;
- local status labels and local Cancel buttons.

This is correct safety engineering, but it produces a product limitation:

> a long-running operation belongs to the widget/controller that launched it instead of to the HTDT application/project.

Concrete current behavior:

- Optimize `before_deactivate()` refuses workspace navigation while search/extended-search or REW workers are active;
- prediction/search workers are parented to local controllers/windows;
- worker shutdown may detach a still-running thread to module-level ownership, but the user then has no application-level place to see that operation;
- #478 already notes that solver execution should not be tied to a disposable Room widget.

As prediction, robustness, project import/export, Capture ingestion, backup, integrity verification and solver execution grow, this workspace-local model will become increasingly restrictive.

## Goal

Introduce an application-scoped **Activity Center / Operation Manager**.

The user should be able to:

- start a safe background operation;
- navigate elsewhere;
- see it in one persistent place;
- cancel when supported;
- inspect failure/retry state;
- return to its originating object/workspace;
- understand whether it is still relevant to the current SceneRevision/project.

This is an orchestration/read-side layer, not a new scientific authority.

## Operation classes

Initial candidates:

### Compute
- prediction execution (#478);
- candidate generation;
- robustness evaluation;
