## Summary

GUI-design review of current `main@6a463b6fa4f4bd85ac3a944938004f6a9cfc80e3` found that HTDT has started to establish a strong global dark theme, but its **scientific plot language is still only minimally specified**.

The current Measurements plot helper in `measurement_page_workspace.py` standardizes only a few fundamentals:

- dark canvas;
- light grid;
- logarithmic X axis;
- axis text colors;
- measured vs predicted color;
- measured = solid, predicted = dashed.

That is a good foundation, but HTDT is becoming increasingly plot-heavy:

- imported FR preview;
- quality inspection;
- measured vs predicted comparison;
- difference plots;
- target curves (#503);
- phase/timing (#489);
- multi-seat comparison (#510/#531);
- calibration;
- optimization;
- acoustic-field slices (#517);
- treatment comparison;
- future commissioning/health drift.

Without a shared visualization contract, each feature will otherwise invent its own:

- colors;
- line styles;
- legends;
- cursor behavior;
- axis formatting;
- selected trace emphasis;
- target display;
- uncertainty display;
- stale/unsupported presentation.

This issue defines a reusable **scientific visualization design system** for 2D engineering plots.

It is a presentation/design issue. It does not change numerical authority.

## Current concrete implementation

`_set_plot_appearance(plot)` currently provides:

```text
background
grid alpha
log X
axis pens/text
downsampling
clip-to-view
default padding
```

Current comparison uses:

- measured: solid line;
- predicted: dashed line;
- difference: dotted line.

This is already better than color-only distinction.

However the current plot UI lacks a shared design specification for:

- current/selected vs background traces;
- target curve;
- derived data;
- uncertainty;
- invalid/out-of-band sections;
- repeated seats/channels;
- cursor/readout;
- linked comparison plots;
- zero/reference lines;
- plot toolbar;
- visual export;
- no-data/loading/stale state;
- consistent tick/number precision.

## Design principle

Scientific plots must answer, in this order:

1. **What quantity am I looking at?**
2. **Which trace is which?**
3. **Which evidence class is each trace?**
4. **What is currently selected?**
5. **What range/reference is valid?**
6. **What exact value is under inspection?**

Decorative styling comes after those six.

## 1. Canonical trace grammar

Do not assign meaning by color alone.

### Measured

- solid;
- visually strongest normal evidence trace;
- canonical measured color from `DARK_THEME.scientific.measured`;
- no glow.

### Predicted

- dashed;
- prediction color;
- similar width to measured;
- legend always includes 「予測」.

### Derived

- dash-dot or another unique pattern;
- visually secondary;
- never uses the same pattern as measured/predicted.

### Target

- fine dotted line;
- neutral/accent target color;
- lower saturation than measured trace;
- target identity shown explicitly.

### Baseline / historical

- thinner;
- lower opacity;
- never visually stronger than current selected evidence.

### Selected trace

Selection modifies:
- width;
- opacity;
- optional marker/cursor association.

Selection must **not change evidence meaning**.

### Unselected multi-trace

For multi-seat/multi-channel views:
- reduce opacity slightly;
- preserve line-style/evidence identity;
- selected trace remains clear.

Do not turn every seat/channel into a random bright color.

## 2. Multi-channel / multi-seat color policy

When several traces share the same evidence class, use a bounded categorical palette.

Rules:
- color identifies channel/seat only within the current graph;
- line style still identifies measured/predicted/target/derived;
- do not reuse warning/error semantic colors as ordinary channel colors where avoidable;
- selected item may use stronger saturation/width;
- legend remains mandatory when >1 channel/seat.

For large trace counts:
- do not show 20 equally strong curves;
- use focus + background t

## Purpose

This issue is the **visual/UI execution plan** for the native HTDT desktop application.

It does not replace #118. #118 owns the workflow-first product/IA direction.

This issue freezes the next design layer in enough detail that implementation can proceed without repeatedly making local visual decisions inside individual widgets.

Review basis:

- current main: `6a463b6fa4f4bd85ac3a944938004f6a9cfc80e3`
- PySide6 / Qt Widgets
- PyVista/VTK Room viewport
- dark-first Windows desktop product
- current canonical workspaces: Overview / Room / Measurements / Optimize
- RDC used for this planning pass: **0**

## Design diagnosis

The structural rewrite under #118 is materially better than the old dock-heavy composition. The remaining gap is no longer "add a dark theme".

The current application still visually reads as several local widget groups rather than one coherent design system.

Concrete examples:

### Shell

`WorkflowRail`:
- expanded width 184 px;
- compact width 112 px;
- text-only buttons;
- compact mode only hides the HTDT brand and reduces margins.

There is no icon-led compact representation.

`TopContextBar`:
- text buttons only;
- no overflow/adaptive policy;
- compact mode only hides the title/reduces spacing.

### Room

Current composition is approximately:

```text
global rail
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ top context bar                                            │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ context tool strip                                         │
├──────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬───────────────┤
│ object       │ overlay controls             │ inspector /   │
│ palette      ├──────────────────────────────┤ context panel │
│              │                              │               │
