- one primary next-action button.

It does not yet read as a mature project dashboard.

### Measurements / Optimize

Both have better page-first structure than the legacy GUI, but visual hierarchy still relies heavily on repeated raised cards/forms.

Repeated cards flatten importance:
- task header;
- primary result;
- helper explanation;
- advanced settings;
- status.

All can visually become "another box".

## Product design character

The target is:

> **technical, calm, spatial, high-density where useful, never dashboard-noisy**

Avoid:
- gamer/neon styling;
- glass everywhere;
- excessive pills;
- card-on-card nesting;
- rainbow category colors;
- huge marketing-style typography;
- icon-only mystery controls;
- photorealistic 3D as a requirement.

Prefer:
- neutral graphite surfaces;
- one controlled accent;
- strong spacing rhythm;
- persistent spatial context;
- concise Japanese task wording;
- dense numeric editing where the task benefits from it;
- scientific plots with restrained visual priority.

## 1. Global visual hierarchy

Use five levels only.

### L0 — Canvas / primary work

The thing the user is judging:
- 3D room;
- measurement plot;
- candidate comparison;
- validation result.

This receives the most area and contrast.

### L1 — Navigation

Global destination and local context.

Navigation must be visually quieter than L0.

### L2 — Task controls

Controls needed to complete the current task.

Examples:
- Draw room;
- Add speaker;
- Import REW;
- Generate candidates.

### L3 — Inspector / details

Precise values and selected-object details.

## Summary

Deep GUI-design review of current `main@6a463b6fa4f4bd85ac3a944938004f6a9cfc80e3` found that HTDT's workflow shell/theme is much more coherent than the current visual semantics inside the Room 3D viewport.

The current renderer in `backend/src/htdt/room_viewport.py` uses:

- a sphere when `SceneEntity.size_m == nil`;
- otherwise one generic `pv.Cube`;
- the same neutral geometry color for every current entity;
- the same general edge style;
- optional text labels as the main way to tell entities apart.

That means a speaker, seat, screen, equipment rack, acoustic treatment and generic furniture item are primarily distinguished by box dimensions and text.

For a home-theater design application, this makes the central 3D canvas visually under-semantic even though the underlying model is rich.

## Why this matters

The Room workspace is intended to be viewport-centric.

A user should be able to understand the scene at a glance:

- which boxes are loudspeakers;
- which are seats/listening locations;
- where the screen is;
- which panels are treatments;
- which object is a rack;
- which direction a speaker faces;
- which objects are current vs proposed;
- which object is selected;

without turning on every label or repeatedly clicking objects.

The current generic-box rendering increases visual search cost and makes screenshots/design review look more like a geometry debugger than a finished theater-design product.

## Current concrete behavior

`_entity_mesh(entity)` currently returns only:

```text
size absent -> Sphere
size present -> Cube
```

`render_document()` then renders every entity with the same:

- `DARK_THEME.viewport.geometry` fill;
- common geometry edge color;
- only the selected item gets the accent outline.

Labels are optional.

This is technically clean and authority-neutral, but visually insufficient.

## Design goal

Add a **semantic viewport visual language** that improves recognition while preserving exact geometry/authority boundaries.

This is not a request for photorealistic furniture or a game-engine renderer.

The goal is fast, restrained, professional visual parsing.

## Proposed renderer layer

### 1. Type-aware visual proxies

When no exact display mesh exists, use simple render-only proxies/glyphs appropriate to the entity kind.

Examples:

- speaker/subwoofer: cabinet proxy with a subtle front/baffle direction cue;
- screen/display: thin framed plane rather than a generic deep box;
- listening position: seat/listener glyph or clear receiver marker;
- seat: simplified seat silhouette/proxy;
- acoustic treatment: thin panel with treatment-specific surface cue;
- equipment rack: rack/cabinet proxy;
- reference point: crosshair/marker rather than a generic sphere.

These are **visual proxies only**.

They must not become solver geometry or measured/as-built truth.

### 2. Preserve exact physical envelopes

Where `size_m` is authoritative, the rendered proxy must still communicate the exact bounding envelope.

A stylized speaker must not silently change the speaker's physical size.

Possible approach:

- exact low-opacity envelope;
- semantic proxy/glyph inside/on the envelope;
- selection handles tied to the actual authority geometry.

### 3. Orientation and acoustic aim

For direction-sensitive entities, orientation should be visually legible without opening the inspector.

Examples:

- speaker front-face cue;
- small aim indicator on hover/selection;
- screen front/back side;
- projector throw direction when that authority is present.

Do not show permanent oversized arrows for every object.

Use progressive disclosure.

### 4. Semantic category palette

Use a restrained category palette, not rainbow coloring.

Possible categories:

- room/architecture;
- source/speaker;
- listener/seat;
- display/video;
- treatment/acoustic object;
- infrastructure/rack.

Color must not be the only differentiator.

Shape/glyph/outline/iconography must carry the same category signal.

