## Summary

Deep lifecycle review of current main@6a463b6fa4f4bd85ac3a944938004f6a9cfc80e3 found that HTDT's product loop currently ends at installation/commissioning verification.

Commissioning establishes that the theater was correct at one point in time. A long-lived Digital Twin should also answer:

> Is the installed theater still behaving like the commissioned baseline months or years later?

There is no tracked first-class post-commissioning health / drift verification workflow.

This is distinct from #520 commissioning and #561 dependency invalidation.

## Ordinary causes of drift

No exceptional failure is required:

- speaker/subwoofer moved slightly;
- seat/furniture/treatment changed;
- AVR reset, firmware or service changed settings;
- routing/crossover/calibration preset changed;
- amplifier/device replacement;
- projector light output aged;
- screen/masking alignment changed;
- HVAC/background-noise condition changed;
- physical room state differs from the commissioned condition.

The system must not assume the cause. It should detect evidence-backed change and guide verification.

## Product goal

Freeze one or more exact health baselines from successful commissioning and support repeatable later checks:

commissioned baseline
→ periodic/on-demand verification campaign
→ compatible current observations
→ changed / no material change / indeterminate
→ targeted diagnostic or revalidation action

## Proposed authority

Introduce immutable SystemHealthBaseline, HealthCheckPlan and HealthCheckRun / DriftAssessment authorities.

### Baseline

Pin exact:

- CommissioningRun / accepted As-built authority;
- operating preset/state used;
- measurement campaign and points;
- routing/calibration/settings identity;
- relevant measured acoustic/video metrics;
- instrument/calibration authority;
- tolerance/change-detection policy.

Do not use a moving pointer to “latest project state” as the baseline.

### HealthCheckPlan

Define a bounded repeatable check set, for example:

- one/few reference FR/IR positions;
- routing/channel sanity;
- selected level/delay checks;
- background-noise check;
- projector luminance check;
- critical as-built dimensions/pose;
- settings snapshot/read-back where available.

A normal health check should be smaller than full commissioning.

### HealthCheckRun

Retain exact new observations and acquisition context.

Compare only semantically compatible evidence.

Do not call two curves “drift” if microphone position, room state, routing, processing or instrument authority makes them non-comparable.

## Drift semantics

Avoid one generic healthy/unhealthy score.

Per check support states such as:

- WITHIN_BASELINE / NO_MATERIAL_CHANGE;
- CHANGED;
- INDETERMINATE;
- NOT_COMPARABLE;
- NOT_RUN.

Always retain the actual observable/delta.

## Do not infer cause from correlation

For example, a material 20–80 Hz response change does not prove that a subwoofer moved or a crossover changed.

HTDT may suggest diagnostic checks through #469/#561, but cause stays hypothesis until supported by evidence.

## Repeatability / uncertainty

Change detection must account for:

- measurement uncertainty;
- repeatability;
- position tolerance;
- operating/environmental state.

Tiny numerical differences are not automatically meaningful drift.

Where repeatability authority is insufficient, return INDETERMINATE.

## Trigger modes

Support:

- manual “Check system health”;
- after equipment/service change;
- after accepted room/installation change;
- optional user-defined periodic reminder later.

No cloud/background monitoring is required for MVP.

## UX

Overview should show the exact commissioned baseline, last verification, changed checks and non-comparable checks, with direct navigation to the reason and targeted follow-up.

Do not show alarming “bad health” status simply because no recent check exists.

## Relationship to existing issues

#520 establishes the commissioning baseline.

#561 explains revalidation

## Summary

Deep video-workflow review of current `main@6a463b6fa4f4bd85ac3a944938004f6a9cfc80e3` found a home-theater-specific gap between the existing projector/screen geometry authority and real content presentation.

Current `cad_video_geometry.py` models one `ScreenGeometryBinding` with:

- one visible width;
- one visible height;
- one image-center offset;
- frame clearance;
- acoustically-transparent state.

A `VideoGeometryRequest` therefore evaluates one fixed image aperture at a time.

`ProjectorSpecification` can declare multiple supported aspect ratios, but there is no first-class authority for **how the theater presents different content aspect ratios in normal operation**.

This matters for common installations such as:

- constant-image-height (CIH) 2.39:1 screen;
- constant-image-width (CIW) 16:9 screen;
- powered/manual masking;
- lens-memory presets;
- zoom/shift changes between 16:9 and scope;
- anamorphic-lens modes;
- subtitles/UI safe-area compromises;
- per-presentation luminance differences caused by image area.

Related: #455 and #557.

## Why one fixed aperture is not enough

A theater may have one physical screen frame but multiple valid **presentation apertures**.

Example:

```text
Physical screen: 2.39:1
Scope movie:
  uses full width and height
16:9 content:
  narrower image
  side masking
  different projector zoom/lens-memory state
```

The physical screen entity did not change.

The operating presentation did.

If HTDT models this by editing the screen dimensions back and forth, it loses the distinction between:

- physical screen construction;
- content presentation aperture;
- masking state;
- projector optical preset.

It also makes #557 photometric calculations ambiguous because screen/image area is mode-dependent.

## Proposed authority

Add immutable/versioned **VideoPresentationProfile** / **PresentationMode** authority.

### 1. Physical screen vs image aperture

Keep physical screen/frame geometry in Scene.

A presentation profile defines the active image aperture inside that physical screen:

- aspect ratio;
- image width/height or deterministic sizing rule;
- center/offset;
- masking state;
- overscan/safe-area only when explicitly modeled.

Do not rewrite physical screen geometry when content mode changes.

### 2. Sizing strategies

Support explicit modes such as:

- CIH — constant image height;
- CIW — constant image width;
- explicit aperture;
- custom/user-defined.

Do not infer a policy solely from screen aspect.

### 3. Masking

Represent:

- none;
- side masking;
- top/bottom masking;
- four-way/custom;
- unknown/manual.

Masking is physical operating state/reference geometry, not a claim about black level.

Where masking panels affect room acoustics, #556 RoomOperatingState can bind the same physical state without merging video and acoustic semantics.

### 4. Projector optical preset

Bind exact projector state where known:

- lens-memory/preset identity;
- zoom;
- lens shift;
- focus state only if representable/evidenced;
- anamorphic lens inserted/removed;
- scaler/aspect mode where relevant.

A preset name alone must not fabricate exact optical parameters.

### 5. Geometry evaluation

Run #455 video geometry against the active presentation aperture.

Per mode evaluate independently:

- throw/zoom feasibility;
- lens shift;
- image containment;
- viewing angle;
- sightline samples;
- collision/clearance where relevant.

### 6. Photometric integration

#557 should calculate expected/measured luminance against the exact active image area and projector mode.

Do not use full physical screen area for a narrower 16:9 image on a scope screen.

### 7. Comparison

Allow a theater design to demonstrate that all required presentation profiles are feasible.

Do not collapse:

- 16:9;
- 1.85:1;
- 2.39:1;

into one overall pass if one mode fails.

### 8. Commissioning

Allow measured confirmation for each important mode:

- actual aperture dimensions;
- projector preset;
- masking posit

