## Summary

Deep home-theater workflow review of current `main@6a463b6fa4f4bd85ac3a944938004f6a9cfc80e3` found no first-class authority for end-to-end **audio/video synchronization**.

HTDT already has several different delay concepts:

- speaker/channel delay and distance in CalibrationPlan (#173/#452);
- source timing/phase authority;
- measurement timing capability;
- multi-channel excitation delay (#492).

Those must not be reused as if they were display-to-audio lip-sync latency.

A home theater can have correctly time-aligned speakers and still have visible A/V sync error from the display/projector/video-processing/audio pipeline.

## Why this is a separate domain

Speaker alignment delay answers:

> When should physical speakers radiate relative to each other?

A/V sync answers:

> When does audible program output arrive relative to the corresponding displayed event?

End-to-end latency can depend on:

- source/input format;
- display/projector processing mode;
- frame rate / refresh mode;
- AVR/video path;
- audio processing mode;
- eARC/HDMI path;
- game/movie/low-latency mode;
- explicit user lip-sync offset.

Changing a display mode can alter A/V latency without changing speaker geometry.

## Proposed authority

Add an immutable `AVSyncCondition` / `AVLatencyMeasurement` authority.

Bind at minimum:

- exact project/SystemVariant/playback chain;
- source/input device/path;
- display/projector identity;
- video mode/refresh/processing state where known;
- audio processing/playback state;
- user-configured lip-sync offset;
- measurement method;
- measured relative audio-vs-video offset;
- uncertainty/repeatability where known;
- timestamp/provenance.

Do not require automatic hardware measurement for MVP.

Manual entry from a trusted external sync test is acceptable when explicitly marked user-measured/imported.

## Workflow

### Commissioning

Add an independent A/V sync check alongside physical/audio commissioning.

Do not mix it into acoustic CalibrationPlan validation.

### Multiple operating modes

Allow distinct records for cases such as:

- Movie mode;
- Game/low-latency mode;
- different frame-rate paths.

The exact operating state should bind to #556 when that authority exists.

### Adjustment

If the device exposes a user lip-sync setting, record:

- before value;
- applied value;
- after measurement.

Exporting/recommending a value does not prove it was applied.

## Semantics

Define a sign convention explicitly, for example:

- positive = audio arrives after video;
- negative = audio arrives before video;

or the reverse, but never leave it UI-specific.

Separate:

- measured end-to-end offset;
- requested correction;
- device setting;
- residual verified offset.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A/V sync latency is a separate authority from per-speaker delay/distance.
- [ ] Exact audio/video operating condition is retained.
- [ ] Multiple projector/display modes can have separate sync measurements.
- [ ] Sign convention and units are canonical.
- [ ] Measurement method/provenance is explicit.
- [ ] Applied lip-sync setting and verified residual are distinct lifecycle states.
- [ ] Commissioning report can include A/V sync without implying acoustic validation.
- [ ] Unknown device/display latency remains UNKNOWN.
- [ ] No proprietary device automation is required for MVP.
- [ ] RDC 0.

