## Summary

Deep static Room/CAD UX review of current main (79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d) found that the workflow-first Room editor is fundamentally a free 3D perspective/orbit viewport.

HTDT can already export top/front/side dimension-sheet data (#453), and #534 plans a floor-plan underlay intended for a top/orthographic workflow, but the actual Room editing surface has no first-class:

- Top / floor-plan view;
- Front elevation;
- Side elevation;
- orthographic/parallel-projection mode;
- deterministic return to saved standard views.

This is a daily CAD usability gap, not merely visual polish.

Related: #118, #453, #491, #481, #534, #537, #455

## Current implementation

RoomViewport3D supports:

- orbit;
- pan;
- zoom;
- fit scene;
- focus entity.

The camera implementation branches correctly when VTK parallel projection happens to be enabled, but no canonical command was found that enables or selects parallel projection.

Source:
https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d/backend/src/htdt/room_viewport.py

The canonical workflow exposes only:

- fit selection;
- fit all;

as Room view commands.

Source:
https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d/backend/src/htdt/workflow_application.py

Repository search found no standard-view commands such as top/front/side, XY/XZ/YZ, or orthographic.

## Why this matters

Home-theater geometry is repeatedly checked in orthogonal planes:

### Floor plan

- speaker/listener X/Y;
- toe-in;
- lateral symmetry;
- walkway clearance;
- room polygon and openings;
- floor-plan tracing (#534);
- cable routing (#538).

### Front elevation

- L/C/R height;
- screen center/edges;
- tweeter/acoustic-center height;
- height speakers;
- projector/screen relationship;
- treatment placement;
- wall clearances.

### Side elevation

- listener ear height;
- screen viewing geometry;
- riser height;
- projector throw/sightline;
- front/rear speaker height;
- ceiling speaker geometry.

A perspective viewport is useful for spatial understanding but inefficient for precise planar placement because perspective distortion makes alignment and dimension comparison harder.

The product already treats top/front/side coordinates as installation-relevant output; users should be able to author and inspect the same geometry in those views.

## Proposed change

Add explicit **Room standard camera/view modes**.

### 1. Standard views

At minimum:

- Perspective / orbit;
- Top;
- Front;
- Rear;
- Left;
- Right.

Top/front/side should use true parallel projection by default.

### 2. Semantic orientation

Standard view labels must resolve against the project's explicit room/front/datum semantics when available.

Coordinate fallback may use HTDT domain axes:

- +X right;
- +Y rear;
- +Z up.

With #537 InstallationDatum, the UI should be able to show whether the view is:

- Scene-axis aligned;
- field-datum aligned.

Do not silently redefine room “front” from current camera direction.

### 3. Editing in orthographic mode

Existing direct manipulation should remain usable:

- select;
- move;
- rotate where semantically meaningful;
- snap;
- ruler;
- object/vertex editing.

An orthographic view is not read-only.

### 4. Plane-aware transform behavior

In a standard view, default manipulation should favor the visible plane:

- Top → XY;
- Front/Rear → XZ;
- Left/Right → YZ.

The user may explicitly override/constrain another axis.

Do not silently modify the hidden coordinate when dragging in a 2D-looking view.

### 5. Precision snapping

Integrate #481:

- grid;
- vertex;
- edge;
- midpoint;
- entity/reference points.

Screen-space tolerance must map correctly under parallel projection and zoom.

