## Summary

Deep static playback-chain review of current main (79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d) found that HTDT's amplifier/headroom authority is deliberately fail-closed around a **single exact resistive load reference**.

That was a sound first slice, but it leaves a major practical limitation for real loudspeakers:

> actual loudspeaker electrical load is frequency-dependent and generally complex/reactive.

Current O100D electrical headroom cannot represent that behavior.

Related: #169, #477, #444, #542, #473

## Current implementation

SpeakerElectricalLoadAuthority supports only:

- exact_resistive_reference;
- nominal_impedance_only;

with one positive resistance_ohm and a valid frequency band.

Source:
https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d/backend/src/htdt/cad_amplifier_headroom.py

The evaluator correctly:

- refuses nominal impedance as exact load evidence;
- converts V↔W only for exact resistive reference using V²/R or sqrt(P·R);
- checks the single resistance against the amplifier's supported load domain.

This avoids fabricating accuracy.

However, a real speaker may present substantially different:

- impedance magnitude;
- electrical phase;
- current demand;
- voltage/current relationship;

across frequency.

A nominal 8-ohm speaker is not an 8-ohm resistor.

## Product impact

Electrical feasibility can change materially with frequency.

Examples:

- impedance minimum around a crossover region;
- high current demand where |Z| is low;
- reactive phase increasing amplifier stress;
- an amplifier wattage rating that is valid at one resistive load but not predictive of a complex loudspeaker load;
- two “8-ohm nominal” speakers with materially different electrical difficulty.

Therefore HTDT cannot yet make a strong broadband claim such as:

> this amplifier/speaker combination has X dB electrical headroom throughout the requested band

from the current scalar load model alone.

## Proposed change

Add a versioned **SpeakerElectricalImpedanceAuthority** and compatible amplifier capability extension.

Keep the existing scalar resistive authority as a valid bounded/simple case.

### 1. Complex load samples

Represent exact frequency-dependent electrical impedance where evidence exists:

- frequency_hz;
- resistance / real(Z);
- reactance / imag(Z);
- or equivalent magnitude + phase with one canonical internal representation;
- valid frequency domain;
- interpolation policy.

Values must be finite and physically interpretable.

Do not infer phase from magnitude-only impedance data.

### 2. Capability tiers

Distinguish:

- UNKNOWN;
- nominal impedance only;
- minimum-impedance metadata;
- magnitude-only impedance curve;
- complex impedance curve;
- exact resistive fixture/reference.

Each tier opens only justified calculations.

### 3. Provenance

Support evidence such as:

- manufacturer published curve/data;
- impedance measurement;
- laboratory/reference dataset;
- user-entered bounded reference;
- inferred/analytic model.

Retain exact source asset/hash and parser/transformation/version where numerical samples are imported.

### 4. EquipmentDefinition binding

Bind exact load authority to EquipmentDefinition ID/version/hash.

A new impedance measurement should be able to exist as a distinct evidence version without silently rewriting historical playback-chain evaluations.

### 5. Amplifier capability extension

Current AmplifierOutputCapability is referenced to a resistive load domain.

To evaluate complex loads, add only evidence-backed constraints the amplifier authority can actually support, for example:

- RMS voltage ceiling;
- peak voltage ceiling;
- RMS/peak current ceiling;
- power/thermal capability with explicit duration/condition;
- stable supported impedance/load region;
- channel-count/shared-supply condition.

Do not derive a current limit from marketing wattage unless an explicit model/evidence supports that conversion.

