## Summary

Deep static measurement-workflow review of current main (79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d) found a usability and evidence-design gap between:

- #472 seat/listener → explicit measurement-point binding;
- #529 guided MeasurementPlan/Campaign execution;
- #510 multi-seat measured analysis.

HTDT can represent individual measurement points and campaigns can consume exact targets, but there is no first-class way to define a **reproducible multi-point microphone sampling pattern** around an MLP/seat/listening region.

This forces users to manually create and position every microphone point for common multi-position campaigns.

Related: #172, #472, #529, #510, #471, #468, #83

## Why this matters

Many practical room-measurement/calibration workflows deliberately sample a spatial region rather than one exact ear point.

Examples include:

- one MLP reference point plus nearby offsets;
- left/right/up/down/forward/back samples around one listener;
- small 3D point clouds around multiple seats;
- repeated verification at a fixed spatial pattern;
- calibration vs holdout points drawn from different predeclared offsets.

Without an explicit pattern authority, users must manually duplicate coordinates and remember:

- which point belongs to which seat;
- which offset convention was used;
- measurement order;
- whether the second campaign used the same geometry;
- whether a point was calibration, holdout or diagnostic;
- whether a moved seat invalidated the old pattern.

That weakens reproducibility even when each resulting measurement is immutably stored.

## Current boundary

#472 already requires a seat listener reference and an explicit action to create a measurement point from it.

That is the correct primitive.

#529 executes exact planned channel × target × repeat cells.

This issue adds the missing **target-set authoring layer**; it does not build another measurement engine.

## Proposed change

Introduce immutable **MeasurementTargetPattern / SpatialSamplingPattern** authority.

### 1. Exact anchor

A pattern should anchor to one exact semantic reference such as:

- seat listener/ear reference;
- existing measurement point;
- explicit world/datum point;
- bounded listening region.

Pin:

- SceneRevision;
- anchor entity/reference semantics;
- exact anchor world position;
- pattern version/hash.

### 2. Deterministic offsets

Store target offsets explicitly in a declared coordinate frame, for example:

- room/world XYZ;
- listener-local left/right/forward/up;
- explicit orientation frame.

Do not infer “forward” from a seat body box if listener orientation is unknown.

### 3. Pattern types

MVP can support a small transparent set:

- single point;
- explicit manual offset list;
- line;
- planar grid;
- bounded 3D grid/cloud;
- symmetric nearby-point preset.

Presets must materialize into explicit offsets and pattern version.

Do not encode proprietary AVR room-correction microphone layouts as hidden magic patterns.

### 4. Point identity and lineage

Materializing a pattern creates exact MeasurementPoint targets with lineage:

- pattern ID/hash;
- anchor identity;
- offset index;
- exact offset;
- creation SceneRevision.

The generated point remains a distinct measurement target.

Historical measurements never move when the anchor seat later moves.

### 5. Rebase/update semantics

If the seat/listener anchor moves:

- existing materialized targets remain historical;
- UI shows their old anchor revision;
- user may explicitly create a **new pattern version/rebased target set** around the current seat.

Do not silently translate historical evidence.

### 6. 3D preview

Before materialization show:

- anchor;
- target points;
- numbering/order;
- offset dimensions;
- room/collision context.

Allow editing offsets numerically and, where practical, through the viewport.

### 7. Tolerance / placement guidance

Each target may optionally carry an explicit microphone placement tolerance/radius.

Tolerance is guidance/

