Deep static source-model review of current main (79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d) found that HTDT now has three useful but disconnected source concepts:

1. normalized frequency-dependent angular directivity;
2. scalar/reference-condition sensitivity in EquipmentDefinition;
3. an exact complex wave-source excitation authority expressed as equivalent monopole volume velocity.

What is missing is a product-level contract and native workflow for the **frequency-dependent source output/response actually used by broadband prediction**, with explicit mapping to the exact solver quantity.

This is required by #101, which explicitly separates “source excitation / level / phase reference” from frequency-dependent directivity.

Related: #101, #168, #409, #414, #444, #457, #477, #492, #540

## Current state

### Directivity is directional shape, not source response

R110 directivity is intentionally normalized directional authority. A DirectivityDataset can describe angular variation at each frequency, but it does not by itself establish the source's absolute on-axis/free-field output spectrum.

That separation is correct.

### Sensitivity is not a complete spectrum

EquipmentDefinition contains SensitivityReference with explicit input/reference semantics.

R110 correctly preserves it as electrical sensitivity/reference evidence, but also explicitly refuses to reinterpret it as wave-source normalization.

Source:
https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d/backend/src/htdt/cad_r110_source.py

### Exact wave excitation exists

AcousticWaveExcitationAuthority already represents:

- exact complex volume-velocity samples;
- valid frequency domain;
- phasor convention;
- interpolation/provenance;
- exact EquipmentDefinition binding.

It explicitly says this quantity is **not inferred from electrical sensitivity**.

Source:
https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d/backend/src/htdt/cad_wave_excitation.py

#409 strengthens the evidence integrity behind that authority, but does not provide a normal user-facing source-response/excitation workflow.

### R150 remains relative-energy transport

The current deterministic R150 path model uses:

- 1 / path_length²;
- source directivity magnitude;
- reflection/scattering factors;

to produce relative energy transport.

That quantity is intentionally not an absolute broadband loudspeaker response.

Source:
https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/79262f50777f61e79d12a3be2afdbbb2d5a2680d/docs/R150_DETERMINISTIC_GA_ADAPTER.md

## Product impact

A real speaker's output is frequency dependent even on-axis.

Two loudspeakers can have:

- similar directivity;
- similar nominal sensitivity at one reference condition;
- very different low-frequency extension;
- different crossover/driver response;
- different free-field magnitude shape;
- different phase response;
- different maximum usable band.

Without an explicit frequency-dependent source-response/excitation contract, HTDT risks one of two bad outcomes:

1. normalized transfer/path results are shown as though they represent the complete speaker response; or
2. each solver invents its own private interpretation of sensitivity/source strength.

Neither is acceptable for reproducible measured-vs-predicted or multi-speaker work.

## Proposed change

Introduce a solver-neutral **SourceResponseAuthority / AcousticSourceOutputAuthority** layer plus native Equipment/Source Library UX.

The exact name is flexible.

### 1. Separate source response from directivity

Represent frequency-dependent source output independently from angular directivity.

Possible evidence tiers:

- UNKNOWN;
- relative on-axis magnitude response;
- absolute free-field SPL response with explicit electrical/acoustic input reference;
- complex free-field transfer with phase;
- exact acoustic volume-velocity/source-strength aut

