- which physical/model parameters were allowed to change;
- what their prior/bounds were;
- which calibration measurements were used;
- what fitting algorithm/objective was used;
- what fitted configuration resulted;
- whether parameters were identifiable;
- which exact model hash was frozen before holdout.

#83 is the owned-room O60 campaign/evidence gate. It does not define a parameter-fitting lifecycle.

## Why this matters

Without a distinct calibration authority, a future implementation can easily collapse several different concepts:

1. microphone/electronic calibration;
2. DSP/AVR CalibrationPlan (#452);
3. O70 statistical residual correction;
4. physical/acoustic model parameter fitting;
5. independent validation.

Those must remain separate.

A room model may need calibration for uncertain quantities such as:

- boundary impedance/material-family parameters;
- inferred porous-material parameters;
- source strength/reference parameters where evidence permits;
- limited scattering/late-field parameters when the provider supports them;
- environment parameters where not independently measured.

But a good fit can be non-unique. For example, several wall/source parameter combinations may produce similar FR residuals.

HTDT must be able to say:

> Predictive fit is acceptable for this observable/band, but physical parameters are not uniquely identified.

