## Summary

Static product/installation-UX review of current `main` (`01c5f81dbfe69ec885d6f494589dd7b84e5914c8`) found that HTDT correctly stores geometry in SI meters, but the native Room editor and installation output expose meters almost everywhere with no presentation/input unit preference.

This is safe internally but awkward for real measurement/installation work, where millimeters or centimeters are often the natural working unit and imperial units may be needed for some equipment/install documentation.

Related: #118, #453, #455, #481, #491

## Current UI

Room Inspector metric fields use:

```python
field.setSuffix(" m")
```

for object position/dimensions.

Room geometry editing likewise uses meter-only fields and text for:

- room height;
- vertex coordinates;
- edge length;
- wall thickness;
- opening offset/width/sill/height.

Sources:

- [room_workspace.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/01c5f81dbfe69ec885d6f494589dd7b84e5914c8/backend/src/htdt/room_workspace.py)
- [room_geometry_panel.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/01c5f81dbfe69ec885d6f494589dd7b84e5914c8/backend/src/htdt/room_geometry_panel.py)

## Current installation output

Installation CSV deliberately uses explicit SI columns such as:

- `x_m`;
- `y_m`;
- `z_m`;
- `mounting_height_m`;
- physical dimensions in meters.

That is good for machine-readable reproducibility.

The HTML report also renders dimensions in meters.

Source:
[report.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/01c5f81dbfe69ec885d6f494589dd7b84e5914c8/backend/src/htdt/report.py)

## Product impact

Common installation values become unnecessarily error-prone in meter-only UI:

- 25 mm wall clearance → 0.025 m;
- 75 mm acoustic panel → 0.075 m;
- 120 mm seat movement → 0.120 m;
- 2500 mm screen width → 2.500 m.

This increases transcription and decimal-place mistakes during:

- survey entry;
- speaker placement;
- treatment placement;
- projector/screen setup;
- riser/seat layout;
- installation handoff.

The fix should **not** change canonical units or hashes.

## Proposed change

Add a user-level **display/input unit preference** while keeping all persisted domain authority in SI units.

### 1. Canonical storage remains unchanged

Continue storing/hashing geometry in:

- meters;
- square meters where applicable;
- seconds;
- SI acoustic/electrical units.

No SceneRevision / SearchSpec / Prediction identity may depend on the user's display-unit preference.

### 2. Length presentation options

At minimum:

- m;
- cm;
- mm.

Optionally support imperial presentation:

- in;
- ft + in;

where it can be implemented without ambiguous parser behavior.

### 3. Input conversion

Spin boxes/forms should accept/display the selected presentation unit and convert at the UI/domain boundary.

Examples:

- `120 mm` → exactly the same domain value as `0.12 m`;
- changing preference from mm to m must not dirty the Scene.

### 4. Precision policy

Choose display decimals per unit so conversion does not silently destroy meaningful precision.

Editing a value after unit conversion must round only according to an explicit UI precision policy, not because the visual string has fewer digits.

### 5. Ruler / constraints / geometry

Use the same unit presentation in:

- Room Inspector;
- geometry panel;
- ruler (#491);
- constraints (#486);
- projector/sightline UI (#455);
- treatment placement (#451).

Avoid each panel implementing its own conversion.

### 6. Installation output

Keep machine-readable CSV SI columns stable.

For human-readable HTML/report output, allow a selected presentation unit or dual display, e.g.:

```text
2,500 mm (2.500 m)
```

The exact SI value remains embedded in the machine-readable authority.

### 7. Project vs application preference

Display units should normally be an application/user preference, not physical project truth.

If a report records a presentation preference, store it as renderi

## Summary

Static product/UX review of current `main` (`a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566`) found that HTDT has a rich, exact `DirectivityDataset` authority but no tracked native workflow for visually validating imported/directivity data before using it for coverage, R110 source compilation or optimization.

This complements #444. #444 makes EquipmentDefinition/directivity creation/import reachable; this issue makes the imported source authority inspectable enough to catch semantic/axis mistakes.

Related: #168, #444, #169, #470, #476

## Existing authority

`DirectivityDataset` stores an exact regular grid over:

- frequency;
- horizontal/azimuth angle;
- vertical/elevation angle;
- magnitude;
- optional coherent phase.

It also freezes:

- coordinate system;
- reference axis;
- azimuth/elevation sign convention;
- normalization;
- valid frequency/angular domain;
- interpolation method/provenance;
- source asset/hash.

Source:
[cad_directivity.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566/backend/src/htdt/cad_directivity.py)

The evaluator can already request exact/interpolated magnitude or complex directivity at a frequency/angle.

## Why a capability badge is not enough

A dataset can be structurally valid and still be semantically wrong for the intended loudspeaker because of source/import interpretation mistakes such as:

- left/right azimuth reversed;
- elevation sign reversed;
- reference/front axis misunderstood;
- polar data rotated by 90°/180°;
- wrong normalization;
- obvious discontinuity or malformed source data;
- valid band much narrower than expected;
- magnitude-only data accidentally expected to support coherent phase.

Those mistakes directly affect:

- coverage;
- source-to-seat off-axis loss;
- aim optimization;
- worst-seat objectives;
- geometric prediction;
- hybrid/source authority.

The user should be able to inspect the imported physical meaning before binding it to a real speaker.

## Proposed change

Add a **Directivity Inspector** inside the Equipment / Source Library from #444.

### 1. Polar slices

At a selected frequency show at minimum:

- horizontal polar;
- vertical polar.

Mark:

- 0° reference axis;
- front/back;
- left/right;
- up/down;
- valid angular domain.

### 2. Frequency sweep

Allow switching frequency or selecting common frequencies from the actual dataset grid.

If interpolation is being previewed, identify it explicitly; do not make interpolated values look like raw samples.

### 3. Angle/frequency heatmap

Provide a compact magnitude heatmap for useful slices so gross discontinuities/axis errors are visible.

### 4. Complex-capability view

For complex datasets, optionally expose phase at selected angular slices.

Magnitude-only datasets must visibly state that coherent phase is unavailable.

### 5. Source geometry linkage

Preview the EquipmentDefinition cabinet/acoustic reference and reference axis next to the directivity orientation.

When bound to a Room speaker, allow a read-only “show this source pattern/orientation in Room” overlay.

Do not silently rotate the dataset to fit the cabinet.

### 6. Provenance

Show concise normal-view fields:

- source format;
- evidence kind;
- valid band;
- angular coverage;
- dataset tier.

Advanced view can expose:

- asset hash;
- interpolation implementation/version;
- normalization details;
- phase reference;
- exact sample-grid metadata.

### 7. Import confirmation

For imported datasets, provide an explicit semantic confirmation step such as:

- front axis looks correct;
- horizontal/elevation convention reviewed.

This is user confirmation/provenance, not numerical validation of the manufacturer data.

## Acceptance

- [ ] a saved DirectivityDataset can be opened from the native Equipment/Source Library.
- [ ] horizontal and vertical polar slices can be inspected at an exact dataset frequency.
- [ ] front/reference axis and angle-sign conventions are visible.

## Summary

Static architecture/product review of current `main` (`a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566`) found that HTDT has strong **single-source** wave/source authority, but it does not yet have an explicit system-level excitation scenario for coherent playback from multiple speakers/subwoofers.

This leaves a major gap between per-source prediction and actual home-theater playback semantics.

Related: #142, #169, #473, #452, #474, #101

## Existing authority

### AcousticSceneSnapshot can contain multiple source definitions

Snapshot v3 has:

- `sources: tuple[AcousticSceneSourceBinding, ...]`;
- one optional `WaveSourceExcitationBinding` per source.

Source:
[cad_acoustic_snapshot.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566/backend/src/htdt/cad_acoustic_snapshot.py)

### WaveSourceExcitationBinding is source-strength authority, not playback routing

`AcousticWaveExcitationAuthority` represents an absolute complex equivalent-monopole volume-velocity spectrum at one EquipmentDefinition acoustic reference.

It does not encode an AVR/DSP playback scenario such as:

- which channels are active together;
- relative gain;
- delay;
- polarity;
- crossover/filter;
- bass-management routing;
- LFE/main-channel relationship.

Source:
[cad_wave_excitation.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566/backend/src/htdt/cad_wave_excitation.py)

### Current R130 wave execution is single-source

`CandidateWaveExecutionInput` has singular fields:

- `source_entity_id`;
- `r110_compiled_source_sha256`;
- one wave-excitation binding;
- one wave-excitation authority.

The deterministic PFFDTD compiler emits exactly one source in its solver input.

Source:
[cad_candidate_wave_execution.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566/backend/src/htdt/cad_candidate_wave_execution.py)

## Existing specification already requires this distinction

#142 explicitly requires:

> channel-independent predictionとmulti-channel excitation scenarioを分離

#169 also requires:

> multi-channel合成は明示scenarioと位相/timing capabilityに従う。per-channel値の単純加算をsystem SPLにしない。

Those are correct requirements, but there is no dedicated authority/implementation slice tracking them.

## Why this matters

Real home-theater questions are often multi-source:

- dual/quad subwoofer integration;
- mains + sub crossover behavior;
- center + bass-managed sub output;
- stereo summation at a seat;
- multi-channel reference-level playback;
- delay/polarity optimization;
- before/after DSP calibration.

Independent per-source magnitude FRs cannot simply be added in dB.

Correct combination may require:

