## Summary

Static UX regression review of current `main` (`a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566`) found that the workflow-first Room transform path no longer exposes the precision snapping semantics required by N20a/N20b (#47/#49).

The current transform controller supports axis restriction, but no position-grid snap, angle snap, or geometric vertex/edge/midpoint/alignment snap is present in the canonical Room path.

Related: #47, #49, #55, #118, #480

## Prior required semantics

#47 required:

- independent grid snap for translation;
- independent angle snap for rotation;
- axis constraint;
- snap settings distinct from geometry tolerance.

#49 extended that with:

- vertex / edge / midpoint / alignment candidates;
- screen-space acquisition/hysteresis;
- deterministic candidate priority;
- geometric snap → grid fallback;
- visible snap target/type/axis feedback;
- modifier + toolbar enable/disable.

These are normal CAD usability requirements, not rare edge-case behavior.

## Current workflow-first implementation

`RoomEntityTransformController` currently implements:

- start move / rotate;
- axis X/Y/Z restriction;
- preview;
- commit/cancel.

Its translation path directly previews the candidate position, with axis components optionally frozen. Its rotation path applies the raw drag angle around the active/default axis.

There is no snap configuration or snap-candidate resolution in this controller.

Source:
[room_transform_input.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566/backend/src/htdt/room_transform_input.py)

The current `cad_input.py` handles input/navigation mechanics but does not provide the N20b geometric snap engine to this workflow-first path.

Source:
[cad_input.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/a597c66b4f5902381f370ce6f24ca56bfb737566/backend/src/htdt/cad_input.py)

## Product impact

Without snapping, common theater-authoring tasks require manual numeric cleanup:

- align FL/FR fronts to the same plane;
- center a speaker on a wall segment;
- place seats on a row;
- align a screen/projector centerline;
- place treatment at exact wall edges/midpoints;
- maintain exact 50/100 mm installation increments;
- rotate cabinets to repeatable angles.

The loss becomes more significant as Room gains richer geometry (#456/#464), because there are more meaningful edges/vertices/surfaces to snap to.

## Proposed change

Restore precision snap as a first-class Room editing capability, using one canonical snap engine shared by viewport transforms.

### 1. Basic snap

Provide independent controls for:

- position grid;
- rotation angle;
- axis constraint.

Example defaults may be user-configurable, but persistence should store the settings as view/editor state rather than Scene physical truth.

### 2. Geometric snap

Support at minimum:

- room/object vertex;
- edge nearest point;
- midpoint;
- alignment to another object/reference.

Do not snap moving entities to themselves.

### 3. Group-aware behavior

Coordinate with #480:

- snap the group/pivot as a unit;
- preserve internal relative positions;
- do not independently quantize each member.

### 4. Feedback

During drag show:

- snap target;
- target type;
- constrained axis/plane;
- resulting exact delta/value.

Users must be able to understand why the object landed at a particular coordinate.

### 5. Override

Allow temporary snap disable for fine/manual movement and a persistent toolbar/settings toggle.

### 6. Determinism / DPI

Keep N20b's logical-pixel candidate selection and hysteresis principles so acquisition behavior remains stable across DPI scale.

