- `riser`
- `screen`

as physical entity kinds.

- [cad_scene.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/7898fa2ff4c4794ade3b7cd5563bac796dcefe11/backend/src/htdt/cad_scene.py)

`cad_video_geometry.py` already implements:

- immutable ProjectorSpecification + provenance;
- throw ratio;
- optical axis / lens reference;
- zoom;
- horizontal/vertical lens shift;
- visible screen aperture/aspect;
- seat viewing angle;
- sightline samples/head obstruction;
- riser interaction;
- speaker/screen/projector collisions;
- acoustically-transparent screen state as separate UNKNOWN/PASS/FAIL semantics.

- [cad_video_geometry.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/7898fa2ff4c4794ade3b7cd5563bac796dcefe11/backend/src/htdt/cad_video_geometry.py)

But the current Room object palette only offers:

- speaker
- seat
- screen
- furniture
- AV equipment
- measurement point

There is no projector or riser action.

- [room_workspace.py](https://github.com/bolph71656-ai/Home-Theater-Digital-Twin/blob/7898fa2ff4c4794ade3b7cd5563bac796dcefe11/backend/src/htdt/room_workspace.py)

There is also no canonical Room/Optimize UI for ProjectorSpecification or VideoGeometryRequest/Evaluation.

## Impact

The current product can model speaker/listener geometry but cannot complete a normal projector-based home-theater layout through the native workflow.

Users cannot answer in HTDT:

- Can this projector make the requested image size at this position?
- Is required lens shift within the exact model capability?
- What viewing angle does each seat get?
- Does a front row block a rear-row sightline?
- How high must a riser be?
- Does the projector/screen collide with speakers or other installation geometry?

This is a major practical layout gap, not a rare numerical edge case.

## Proposed change

### 1. Room entities

Add first-class palette actions for:

- Projector
- Riser

with the same direct-manipulation/gizmo/Undo/save semantics as other physical entities.

### 2. Projector specification library

Allow binding an exact ProjectorSpecification:

- manufacturer/model or user-defined;
- throw ratio range;
- optical/lens reference;
- supported aspect;
- zoom/lens-shift limits;
- physical cabinet size;
- provenance/source.

Reuse existing authority and #415 provenance requirements.

### 3. Screen geometry

Extend the screen inspector with:

- visible image width/height;
- aspect ratio;
- frame clearance;
- image-center offset;
- acoustically-transparent state: yes/no/unknown.

Do not treat acoustic transparency as proof of a validated acoustic transfer effect.

### 4. Seat / riser bindings

Expose:

- eye point;
- head/obstruction radius;
- row/riser height where applicable;
- sightline policy samples.

### 5. 3D overlays

Render optional overlays for:

- projection cone;
- image aperture;
- optical axis;
- required lens shift;
- viewing-angle envelope;
- blocked sightlines;
- collision pairs.

### 6. Evaluation and comparison

Provide explicit evaluation from Room and include exact video-geometry status in named/SystemVariant comparisons.

Keep each criterion independent; do not create an overall theater score.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] User can add/edit a projector and riser in Room without scripts.
- [ ] User can create/select an exact ProjectorSpecification and bind it to the projector entity.
- [ ] Screen visible aperture/aspect/frame metadata are editable separately from the generic screen box.
- [ ] Seat eye/head and riser geometry can be configured for sightline evaluation.
